FOUND=[
("Set in the past now.","81 of the 233 films (35%) take place in a year that has already arrived. The median film looks 34 years ahead."),
("About 1 in 5 predictions came true","when near-misses count as half (20.2% of 220 scored forecasts). Only about 1 in 9 came true outright (11.4%)."),
("Communication tech won by a mile.","Video calls, flat screens and voice control scored 57.5%, far ahead of any other theme. Society and politics (11%) and transport (13%, from just 12 forecasts) did worst."),
("Bleak futures were no better guides.","Strictly dystopian films scored 19% against 22% for the rest: no meaningful difference (p = 0.14)."),
("Gadgets right, catastrophes wrong.","Gadget forecasts scored 41%, but only 2 of 42 plagues, wars and collapses happened (both a second world war, foreseen in 1929 and 1933)."),
]
METHOD=[
("Films:","released features (cinema or streaming) set in a named future year on humanity's current calendar, every year sourced. Shorts only fill early gaps; alternate histories and invented calendars are excluded."),
("Setting year:","taken from on-screen dates or official sources; ambiguous, derived or off-screen years are flagged *. Gap = setting year minus release year."),
("Predictions:","the main on-screen technology and society forecasts in the 81 films whose year has arrived, scored came true / sort of / didn't by the setting year (later counts as didn't). Tech that already existed at release and plot devices (time travel, aliens, monsters) are excluded."),
("Checks:","I scored everything myself and checked it twice (a random 40 re-scored: 39 agreed; every hit re-checked; the 44 added in the 233-film update checked line by line, 3 changed). Tone from Wikipedia descriptions, cross-checked with a stricter on-screen rule."),
("Caveat:","these are well-known films that name a year, not a random sample of science fiction, and many groups are small."),
]
SOURCES="Sources: Wikipedia, AFI Catalog, BFI, SFE, EOFFTV, DEFA, Screen Rant, Smithsonian and press coverage of individual films. See also Wythoff & Leane 2025; Xu et al. 2021."
